# d27_named_groups / numbering_capture.rxt
#
# PROMISE under test: named groups interleave with unnamed groups in
# ONE left-to-right numbering sequence -- a named group does not get a
# separate number space of its own. Nesting, optional/quantified
# participation, and alternation all number exactly as the unnamed form
# would. Capture DELIVERY is the ordinary caps[k]-is-group-k contract
# (docs/spec/match_api.md S3.3/S5): a named group's capture is read by
# its NUMBER, like any other group -- the frozen match API has no
# name-indexed accessor (see README's "module boundary" note).
#
# Oracle: python3 `re`, on the (?P<name>...) translation, verified
# group-by-group via m.span(k) for every k -- see oracle.py.

# unnamed, named, unnamed -- the named group takes the middle number
# purely by its position, same as an unnamed (b) would
pattern (a)(?<n>b)(c)
features named-groups
m "xabcx" 1 4
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
g 3 3 4

# a named group nested inside another named group, with a plain unnamed
# group in between -- numbering is left-to-right by opening paren,
# independent of which of the three is named
pattern (?<n>(a)(?'m'b))
features named-groups
m "xabx" 1 3
g 1 1 3
g 2 1 2
g 3 2 3

# optional named group: not participating leaves its slot unset,
# exactly like an unnamed optional group would (docs/spec/match_api.md
# S5's unset convention, {-1,-1})
pattern (x)(?<n>y)?(z)
features named-groups
m "xz" 0 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 -1 -1
g 3 1 2

pattern (x)(?<n>y)?(z)
features named-groups
m "xyz" 0 3
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
g 3 2 3

# alternation: a named group in one branch, an unnamed group in the
# other -- still consecutive numbers, and whichever branch was not
# taken leaves its group (named or not) unset. This is ONE name in the
# whole pattern, not a duplicate (contrast duplicates.rxt)
pattern (?<n>x)|(y)
features named-groups
m "x" 0 1
g 1 0 1
g 2 -1 -1

pattern (?<n>x)|(y)
features named-groups
m "y" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 0 1

# cross-iteration retention (docs/spec/match_api.md S5.1's first rule)
# applies to a named group exactly as it does to a numbered one: group
# a1 ran only in the match's first iteration and keeps that span even
# though the LAST iteration took the other (b1) branch
pattern ((?<a1>a)|(?<b1>b))*
features named-groups
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 1 2
g 2 0 1
g 3 1 2

# an empty final iteration still overwrites (S5.1's second rule),
# named-group form: group a1's last iteration matched zero bytes at the
# subject's end, and that write stands
pattern (?<a1>a*)*
features named-groups
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 3 3
